  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Exponential Weighting",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply exponential weighting to a continuous-time signal to determine how multiplying by $e^{-\\sigma t}$ can make a nonconvergent Fourier-transform integral converge and motivate the Laplace-domain variable $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Weighting",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Exponential Weighting",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The move is to use one explicit instruction step: identify when a signal fails the Fourier-transform setup and then add a damping factor to repair convergence before doing further work.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the Fourier-transform template. If we can use $X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$, then we can continue with frequency-response calculations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Signals that do not decay can make that integral diverge, which means the Fourier transform does not exist in the standard sense. In that case, the practical fix is to apply exponential weighting so convergence can be tested through a controllable real parameter.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Define a weighted signal $x_w(t)=x(t)e^{-\\sigma t}$ for $\\sigma>0$. The factor $e^{-\\sigma t}$ is a damping term that reduces high-$t$ growth and creates a direct condition you can tune.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same base case from the outline: with $x(t)=u(t)$, compare $\\int_0^{\\infty}u(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$ to $\\int_0^{\\infty}u(t)e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$. The first one is not absolutely controllable, while the second is finite when $\\sigma$ is chosen positive.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the first step in the Laplace motivation chain: later steps will keep this weighted form, apply it to additional examples, and then rewrite the integral with $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Weighting",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Applying a Decaying Weight to a Nondecaying Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_0^{\\infty}u(t)e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$ and state the condition on $\\sigma$ for convergence.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the weighted integral as the object to evaluate: for a right-sided signal $x(t)$, test $X_\\sigma(j\\omega)=\\int_0^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$. The rule for this section is to use $x(t)=u(t)$ so the algebra is minimal and the lesson move stays visible.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}u(t)e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt = \\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the magnitude test to this expression. Because the oscillatory factor $e^{-j\\omega t}$ has unit magnitude, $\\left|e^{-(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\right|=e^{-\\sigma t}$. Convergence is controlled by the real part exponent, so this becomes a one-sided exponential-tail condition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-\\sigma t}\\,dt=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{\\sigma},& \\sigma>0\\\\ \\infty,& \\sigma\\le 0\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rule is: require $\\sigma>0$ for the weighted signal to be convergent. With that condition, the transformed value is finite and can be written as a standard geometric-type result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\,dt=\\left[\\frac{e^{-(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}}{-(\\sigma+j\\omega)}\\right]_0^{\\infty}=\\frac{1}{\\sigma+j\\omega}, \\quad \\sigma>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result separation: the condition is $\\sigma>0$, and the step output is $\\frac{1}{\\sigma+j\\omega}$. This is the first concrete example of how exponential weighting repairs the nondecaying step signal and defines the real-part parameter used in the Laplace view.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Weighting",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Testing Convergence After Weighting",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Test convergence of $\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-2t}e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$ and derive the inequality on $\\sigma$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the weighted one-sided transform form. Use the same representation as earlier, now with the decaying signal $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ so both inherent decay and the added damping are visible in one integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-2t}e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt=\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-(\\sigma+2+j\\omega)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Combine exponents into a real-plus-imaginary form. The real part is $-(\\sigma+2)$, so the magnitude check uses $\\left|e^{-(\\sigma+2+j\\omega)t}\\right|=e^{-(\\sigma+2)t}$. The oscillatory term $e^{-j\\omega t}$ affects phase only.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-(\\sigma+2)t}\\,dt=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{\\sigma+2},& \\sigma>-2\\\\ \\infty,& \\sigma\\le -2\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the convergence rule for a one-sided exponential tail: require $\\sigma>-2$. That is the full inequality after combining the decay rates from both $e^{-2t}$ and $e^{-\\sigma t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-(\\sigma+2+j\\omega)t}\\,dt=\\left[\\frac{e^{-(\\sigma+2+j\\omega)t}}{-(\\sigma+2+j\\omega)}\\right]_0^{\\infty}=\\frac{1}{\\sigma+2+j\\omega}, \\quad \\sigma>-2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: the weighted transform is finite for $\\sigma>-2$, and the value is $\\frac{1}{\\sigma+2+j\\omega}$. This keeps one new wrinkle from the previous step: we now add the signal decay rate into the real-part exponent instead of learning a fresh rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Weighting",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Choosing a Larger Weight for a Growing Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the condition on $\\sigma$ for $\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{2t}e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$ to converge, and simplify the result.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same weighted one-sided template and insert the growing signal choice $x(t)=e^{2t}u(t)$. The action is to combine all exponentials in the kernel and then apply the one-sided exponential-tail convergence rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{2t}e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt=\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{(2-\\sigma-j\\omega)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the exponent as a real part plus imaginary part: $2-\\sigma-j\\omega$. Convergence on $[0,\\infty)$ depends on the real part only, so we check the sign of $2-\\sigma$. The oscillatory factor is controlled by $j\\omega$ and does not set convergence.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{(2-\\sigma)t}\\,dt=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{\\sigma-2},& \\sigma>2\\\\ \\infty,& \\sigma\\le 2\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set the inequality so the real exponent is negative: $2-\\sigma<0$, which gives the minimum required damping level $\\sigma>2$. This is the first step where the learner must pick a larger damping constant than the growth rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{(2-\\sigma-j\\omega)t}\\,dt=\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{-(\\sigma-2+j\\omega)t}\\,dt=\\left[\\frac{e^{-(\\sigma-2+j\\omega)t}}{-(\\sigma-2+j\\omega)}\\right]_0^{\\infty}=\\frac{1}{\\sigma-2+j\\omega}, \\quad \\sigma>2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: the integral converges only for $\\sigma>2$, and the weighted form becomes $\\frac{1}{\\sigma-2+j\\omega}$. This preserves one workflow and adds the new wrinkle that a growing signal sets a stricter damping threshold.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Weighting",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Combining Exponential Weighting with the Fourier Kernel",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $\\int_0^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$ as $\\int_0^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$ and define $s$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the weighted integral kernel from earlier sections. Keep $x(t)$ general and rewrite only the exponential factors: replace the explicit damping and oscillation pair with one compact exponent.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt=\\int_0^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the exponent-addition identity to combine the exponentials into one real-imaginary decomposition. Then introduce the compact symbol $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$. This is the same rule used before, only now written in reusable $s$-notation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\,dt=\\int_0^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt, \\quad s=\\sigma+j\\omega",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final interpretation: $\\sigma=\\Re\\{s\\}$ is the real-part damping coordinate that determines convergence, and $\\omega=\\Im\\{s\\}$ is the oscillatory coordinate that matches the Fourier frequency term. This preserves the prior mechanics while compressing them into the standard Laplace-kernel form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
